 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 531870,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/0afc87f162359cc0.jpg\" alt=\"Table with column headings, “Mass Extinction,” Time of Extinction,” and “Organisms Greatly Reduced or Made Extinct.” First row: “End of the Ordovician period,” “443 million years ago,” and “Trilobites, brachiopods, echinoderms, and corals.” Second row: “End of the Devonian period,” “354 million years ago,” and “Marine families on tropical reefs, corals, brachiopods, and bivalves.” Third row: “End of the Permian period,” 248 million years ago,” and “Trilobites, mollusks, brachiopods, and many vertebrates.” Fourth row: “End of the Triassic period,” “206 million years ago,” and “Mollusks, sponges, marine vertebrates, and large amphibians.” Fifth row: “End of the Cretaceous period,” “65 million years ago,” and “ Ammonites, dinosaurs, brachiopods, bivalves, and echinoderms.”\"></p>\n\n<p>A dig in a farmland soil rich in calcium carbonate reveals the following findings:\n 1. Numerous shells of bivalves\n 2. Chips of corals\n 3. The partial imprint of a trilobite\n 4. A few vertebrae dated to 250 million years ago.\n The table of extinction summarizes information on the five major extinction events.</p>\n\n<p>According to the table of extinction, which conclusion about the fossils is most reasonable?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1040396,
     "content_html": "The trilobites were the first animals to conquer land."
    },
    {
     "id": 1040397,
     "content_html": "The rocks can be dated to the Cretaceous Cretaceous-Paleogene boundary."
    },
    {
     "id": 1040398,
     "content_html": "The farmland was probably part of the seafloor in the Permian period"
    },
    {
     "id": 1040399,
     "content_html": "The fossils can be dated to the Ordovician period."
    }
   ],
